  it goes without saying that many made  the connection to this concept art where  we see nintendo toying around  with the idea of link's arm transforming  into all kinds of different tools and  weapons  an idea which could have been scrapped  in favor of the sheikah slate until we  learn more about the game this is just a  prediction of course  and the correlation between the imagery  of link's hand and the concept art could  just be a coincidence and nothing more  that being said another assumption  people have made either as a prediction  or  just wishful thinking is the return of  bigger more complex dungeons akin to the  ones we encountered in previous  installments whether or not you  personally count the divine beasts the  shrines and hyrule castle as  actual dungeons yes or no is up to you  miyamoto doesn't seem to regard them as  such but then again  if they felt like dungeons to you then  who are we to say that you are wrong  this video is also not about which is  better the older the new we all have our  preferences but one thing's for sure  breath of the wild's take was definitely  very different from what we were used to  and in this video together with conrad  from the commonwealth realm we want to  take a look at some aspects which may  foreshadow a return to form when it  comes to zelda dungeons these include  hints and clues from breathtawa's  development cycle the sequel's trailer  game design reasons fan feedback and  more so without further ado  here are 5 hints and reasons for the  return of dungeons in breath of the wild  too  breath of the wild saw quite a lot of  changes and revisions during its  development but by far the biggest ones  took place between 2014 when the game  was first announced and the first proper  trailer at e3 in 2016.  it's obvious that between these two  periods in time a lot of crucial  decisions were made about which  direction the game should take  and this is most evident from the 2014  game awards presentation  the first gameplay demonstration  following the announcement of the game  six months prior here we see miyamoto  and aonuma showing off the early  gameplay concepts of breath the wild  which at this point in time  didn't have a name yet the paraglider  which was still a sail cloth at this  point like the one from skyward sword  horse riding fighting enemies and other  little details about the controls in the  game's engine  at some point during the presentation  miyamoto states the following  this looks a bit suspicious we must be  near a dungeon  what's interesting is that aonuma is  clearly exploring an early build of the  farron region at this moment  during his remark miyamoto points at two  owl statues standing side by side  a common occurrence in farren and here's  the thing farren only ended up playing a  very minute role in the game  it has no relevance to the main plot of  the game at all and more importantly  it doesn't feature a divine beast  there's a good chance that at this stage  of development the divine beasts were  either not yet conceptualized or at the  very least one of many ideas the staff  was considering and no decision was yet  made about which direction to take  this is further supported by the  complete absence of the sheikah slate  during this presentation  the slate wasn't introduced until e3 of  2016  more than one and a half years later not  only that but during the presentation  the two are confident that the game will  release somewhere in 2015  and well we all know how that panned out  the sheikah slade and divine beasts are  closely connected story wise as are the  shrines and chica towers none of which  are visible during this presentation  we do see an early concept of using  towers to scout the area but these don't  look anything like the shika towers and  are instead just wooden structures with  a platform on top  so could it be that at this stage of  development the team was still set on  making regular dungeons  ones which were not based on shika  technology and one of them was supposed  to be located in farren somewhere  perhaps  a forest-based dungeon if this is true  then miyamoto's remark about a dungeon  being located in farren could be another  one of those early ideas one which also  ended up being scrapped in favor of the  sheik attack just like the idea of  link's afflicted hand  and might still be on the shelf to be  used for the sequel  the owl statues were present from the  very beginning of breath of the wild  announcement as early as the very first  reveal  so perhaps nintendo had bigger plans for  these statues being tied to a big story  event and subsequently a more classic  dungeon to go with it  and surprise surprise what is one of the  first things we see in the reveal  trailer of breath the wild 2  zonai based architecture of which our  statues are a big part of which brings  us to the next point  the fact that we see a large dark cave  system in the reveal trailer  may seem trivial but is actually a huge  contrast to breath the wild  hyrule was vast and full of secrets  short but large  complex caves were not part of that  mystery in fact  even the biggest caves found in the game  are child's play compared to previous  installments  they rarely if ever branch off into  multiple directions and generally don't  consist of more than one room or  corridor  they also aren't dark by any means and  can be easily explored without the use  of a light source  naturally there is the possibility that  the cable we see in the reveal trailer  is only tied to cutscenes and nothing  more or even completely absent  from the final game but if it's not then  nintendo could be setting us  up for something bigger a recurring  theme of exploring  vast underground locations these don't  necessarily have to be tied to one  single theme  like a cave but also other types of  indoor environments  in fact there is another piece of  concept art which might have  some connection to the whole idea of  venturing underground and solving  mysteries in a dark  dungeon-like setting namely this one  shown at the gdc session  in 2017 by art director satoru takizawa  here we see link investigating an  ancient scripture in what seems to be an  unlit  man-made temple or underground structure  notice how he's also carrying a lantern  something he didn't have  in the final game which might be  included in the sequel speaking of the  sequel  doesn't the setting in this artwork of  lighting up a wall to investigate  ancient writings look a bit  familiar perhaps another scrapped idea  that will make its way into the sequel  in some shape or form  exploring dark ancient crypts to uncover  mysteries from times unknown  there was definitely a lack of enclosed  spaces to explore and brought  the wild yes we had the shrines divine  beasts and castles but the variety and  aesthetics and atmosphere of indoor  environments definitely left a lot to be  desired  the fact that nintendo presents us with  this grand underground cave could again  be a hint of things to come  especially with braftawal's overworld  having been completely comped  out and explored to its full extent  hence more focus on exploring enclosed  spaces  would make sense if they plan to revisit  the same world  something aonuma has already confirmed  will be the case  adding a new province somewhere probably  won't be enough to make the game feel  less than a glorified dlc pack  hence why a plethora of new explorable  dungeonesque locations hidden within the  same overworld map  will be a great way to expand an already  huge  game it would also present a lot more  options for story and gameplay which  leads us into  next aspects  as much as we enjoy the divine beasts  and shrines on our first playthrough  there are a few things that make a  return of the mechanical giants and  puzzle-based mini-challenges  problematic one of which being that the  divine beast  and shrine concept is severely limited  for most people  they were an absolute joy to tackle yet  at the same time they quickly lose steam  over the course of the adventure  especially the divine beasts this is  because they are all based  on the same core idea i would argue that  by the time the champion's ballot came  along  the idea had already overstayed its  welcome and the review scores for this  dlc pack  reflects that sentiment more shrines and  another defined beast like challenge  simply wasn't enough to peak people's  interest we'd already seen the  mechanical giants four times and in the  case of the shrines  120 times the idea had run its course  this problem is a lot less prevalent  than previous titles since the dungeon  variety provided  so much more to work with as late as  2011 in skyward sword  nintendo was still bringing in brand new  ideas never before  seen in the zelda series new locations  new puzzles new ways to traverse a  dungeon and so on  this kind of variation is simply a lot  harder to accomplish with the divine  beasts since they all work the same way  mechanically  and the size although large for a moving  behemoth is still limited to a specific  dimension  inside breath of the wild's overworld  unless they grow to the size of a titan  like the ones from xenoblade chronicles  2 there will always be a massive  restriction on how  big these things can actually get second  is the story implications  breath the while 2 needs to stand out  from its predecessor in order to avoid  feeling like a simple expansion  rather than a proper sequel and i simply  see very little options for the divine  beasts making a return as the game's  central dungeons  again their size is limited and we  already explored them on the inside  extensively there's simply no reason to  go through the same motion  again they may still be around in the  overworld but their inclusion in the  story would have to serve a different  purpose this time around  last but not least the beasts were tied  to the story arc of the champions  which have passed on to the afterlife at  the end of breath the wild  it is possible that the four giants play  another role revolving around the  so-called new  champions if not for the fact that age  of calamity already explored this idea  and will probably feel very repetitive  if this is revisited again  yes some of this is subjective with a  fanbase as big and passionate as the  zelda community there needs to be a  middle ground to keep everyone  interested speaking of the fans  [Music]  despite all the praise breath of the  wild has received since its release  even a great game isn't without its  criticism most changes to the zelda  formula brought forth by the game were a  welcome inclusion but when looking at  the main consensus  similar complaints keep coming back  fragile weapons  rain the lack of story and of course  most importantly  not being allowed to pet dogs what were  you thinking nintendo  among these general complaints the  absence of dungeons is one of those  recurring sentiments the divine beasts  may have been necessary to break the  zelda conventions a breath of fresh air  after decades of doing the same old  routine  yet it's undeniable that the zelda  fanbase was heavily divided on whether  or not the divine beasts were a  satisfying replacement for the dungeons  of  old and here's where we need to keep one  thing in mind nintendo does  listen it may not always seem that way  as nintendo rarely communicates about  these kinds of matters to us directly  but every now and again we are given  hints that aonuma and his team are not  completely oblivious to fan feedback and  demands  as an example director hidomaru  fujibayashi stated in an interview that  quote i think while we were working on  both the main game and the dlc it was a  process of constantly getting lots of  different new ideas as we refined the  game  and finding out new things we wanted to  do even in situations like this  talking to people and finding out that  people want to pet dogs gives me a lot  of motivation  a lot of ideas for things we could put  into the game in turn aunuma also  brought up something similar in an  interview with famitsu  saying that although it's not easy to  answer all the expectations of all  players he does recognize that the  significance and continuation of the  series is found in its fanbase  yes the zelda team listens and since the  lack of proper dungeons still remains a  general complaint it is definitely not  too far out there to assume that for the  sequel they might have taken this  sentiment to heart  breath of the wild was in many ways an  experiment a way to breathe new life  into the series but this doesn't mean  that  every change we saw will be a mainstay  for instance after the release of  skyward sword aunuma made the argument  that moving forward the series would  probably keep motion controls as a  central mechanic  and while motion controls aren't  completely gone in breath of the wild  they definitely took a back seat and are  almost completely optional  and i'm willing to bet that fan feedback  after skyward sword's release  did play a role in this speaking of  skyward sword  [Music]  if breath wall 2 is set for release in  2021  or at the latest in early 2022 then the  release of skyward sword hd  may hold some significance for the  direction of the sequel  out of all zelda games skyward sword is  by far the game that shares the closest  connection to breath the wild the  goddess hylia statues  fire's voice inside the master sword the  free springs which are almost an exact  redesign  of those found in skyward sword the  three elemental dragons  din roll nedra farish and so on  having played for skyward sword will  make it obvious just how much of its  lore was adapted by breath of the wild  and that makes it all the more fitting  that skyward sword hd will be the game  preceding breath the wild 2's launch  this game will essentially serve as the  appetizer to the long-awaited sequel and  here's what really matters  skyward sword arguably has some of the  most original dungeon designs in the  entire series  from the mysterious ancient system to  the pirate ship and its unique time  shift stone mechanic  to skykeep with its ability to shift the  layout of the dungeon itself around  like it or hate it skyward sword had  little to no shortages in the dungeon  department  this might be a good incentive for the  zelda team to include strong and unique  dungeons to breath the wild sequel after  all it might be a bit of a  disappointment for people  if they have just come off of playing a  zelda game with great dungeons only to  have the new game be completely devoid  of dungeons altogether aka  skyward sword might have actually forced  the zelda team  to change up the dungeon formula for the  sequel to avoid  new fans of the series being somewhat  underwhelmed after picking up skyward  sword for the first time  the same thing could be said about story  but that is a topic for another time  and there you have it 5 reasons why  dungeons could make a return in breath  the wild sequel  be sure to share your thoughts in the  comments below are you more in favor of  a return to form or would you rather see  the divine beast concept being expanded  upon  also if you want to see more regarding  the possibilities of breath to wall 2's  dungeons be sure to check out my very  first video  where me and nintendo black crisis  explore some of the ideas and concepts  with that said  thank you to conrad for joining me be  sure to watch his video over on  commonwealth realm where together we  will go more in depth on dungeons in  breathtak 2 so  go check it out thank you all so much  for watching and a big thanks to my  gracious patreon supporters and members  who make these videos possible  also please give a warm welcome to my  latest supporters  magikarp kingdom it's elektra and  squall045  a fine addition to my collection no but  seriously thank you all so much  and that is all for now i hope to see  you next time this is dawn signing off  and have a good one  [Music]  mmm  [Music]  you

